12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1f",null,{"guide":{"date":"September 17, 1862","place":"The northern Antietam battlefield near Sharpsburg, Maryland, from the Joseph Poffenberger farm and Miller’s Cornfield south to the Dunker Church plateau, with the East and West Woods on either flank","result":"Soon after daybreak, Joseph Hooker opened the Battle of Antietam by sending the United States I Corps south toward the Dunker Church. James B. Ricketts’s division attacked through the Cornfield and East Woods in separate brigade actions. Alexander Lawton’s Confederates, supported by Stephen D. Lee’s artillery and a counterattack by Harry T. Hays’s Louisiana brigade, stopped the advance. By about 7 a.m. the fighting in the eastern Cornfield and East Woods had reached a costly stalemate; Abner Doubleday’s simultaneous attack along the Hagerstown Turnpike continues in the next episode","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The opening action at Antietam, from daybreak to about 7 a.m.","note":"The episode follows Hooker’s plan, the opening artillery exchange, and Ricketts’s attack through the eastern Cornfield and East Woods. Doubleday’s attack on the I Corps right is introduced but reserved for the next episode."},{"label":"Hooker’s objective","value":"The Dunker Church plateau","note":"Hooker planned to attack south along the Hagerstown Turnpike and believed possession of the plateau would begin to roll up the Confederate left."},{"label":"United States opening force","value":"About 8,600 men in I Corps in the episode’s estimate","note":"Hooker’s three divisions opened the northern attack before Mansfield’s XII Corps could provide effective support."},{"label":"I Corps deployment","value":"Doubleday right, Ricketts left, Meade supporting the center","note":"Truman Seymour’s brigade of Meade’s Pennsylvania Reserves already held a lodgement in the East Woods after the September 16 skirmish."},{"label":"Hooker’s expanded responsibility","value":"A semi-independent two-corps wing","note":"McClellan expected Hooker to coordinate I Corps and Mansfield’s XII Corps on the northern battlefield, but Hooker planned his own corps attack before sending Mansfield a general order to support it."},{"label":"Coordination failure","value":"I Corps was engaged before XII Corps received useful direction","note":"Mansfield had commanded XII Corps for only two days. The episode says his roughly 10,000 troops required an hour or more to assemble and enter the fight."},{"label":"Confederate opening force","value":"About 7,700 men under Jackson in the episode’s estimate","note":"The force included most of Alexander Lawton’s and John R. Jones’s divisions, with John Bell Hood’s two brigades initially in reserve."},{"label":"Confederate front","value":"About three-quarters of a mile facing north","note":"Lawton’s division covered the ground south of the Cornfield and across the Smoketown Road toward the Mumma farm; Jones’s division occupied the West Woods sector north of the Dunker Church."},{"label":"Mumma farmhouse","value":"Burned by Confederate troops before the main attack","note":"The Confederates destroyed the house so United States sharpshooters could not use it as a strongpoint. Its smoke became a conspicuous landmark over the eastern battlefield."},{"label":"Dunker Church artillery","value":"Four batteries under Col. Stephen D. Lee","note":"Lee’s artillery battalion occupied the open plateau east of the church and fired north and northeast into the approaches used by I Corps."},{"label":"Nicodemus Heights","value":"Fourteen guns under Stuart and Pelham in the episode’s account","note":"The high ground west of the Hagerstown Turnpike allowed Confederate artillery to strike the right flank of the Federal advance. Early’s brigade protected the position."},{"label":"Opening fire","value":"Artillery engaged soon after 5 a.m.","note":"A. W. Garber later claimed the opening shot for his battery on Nicodemus Heights. The episode treats that claim cautiously and describes Confederate and Federal batteries opening as visibility improved."},{"label":"East Woods renewal","value":"Seymour’s Pennsylvania Reserves advanced at first light","note":"Three regiments pushed to the southern edge of the woods and fired on James A. Walker’s Confederates along the Mumma farm, renewing the fight that darkness had interrupted."},{"label":"Ricketts’s lead brigade","value":"About 1,100 men under Abram Duryea","note":"Duryea’s New York and Pennsylvania regiments entered the head-high corn at about 6 a.m. without the close support Ricketts intended."},{"label":"Confederate first line","value":"Douglass’s Georgians south of the Cornfield","note":"Marcellus Douglass ordered his men to lie down in the rolling pasture and wait. They rose and fired when Duryea’s troops emerged from the southern edge of the corn."},{"label":"Converging Confederate fire","value":"Douglass in front and Walker from the east","note":"Walker shifted part of Trimble’s brigade across the Smoketown Road to a rocky ledge. The episode says only about 40 of the 12th Georgia’s 100 men remained unhurt after the exchange."},{"label":"Ricketts’s broken sequence","value":"Three brigades attacked piecemeal","note":"George Hartsuff’s brigade was delayed after Hartsuff was wounded by a shell fragment, while William Christian abandoned command of the other supporting brigade. Richard Coulter and Peter Lyle brought those formations forward separately."},{"label":"Duryea’s loss","value":"Nearly one-third of the brigade in about 30 minutes","note":"Low ammunition and mounting pressure forced Duryea back through the Cornfield. The brigade withdrew in order but did not fight again that day."},{"label":"Hays’s counterattack","value":"Louisiana troops struck Coulter’s brigade near the Cornfield","note":"The Louisiana brigade and parts of Douglass’s brigade drove the Federals toward the corn and East Woods before artillery and infantry fire forced the Confederates back."},{"label":"12th Massachusetts","value":"224 killed or wounded of 334 engaged, about 67 percent","note":"The National Park Service identifies this as the highest percentage loss of any United States regiment at Antietam. The episode describes only 32 men initially remaining with the colors when the regiment withdrew."},{"label":"Louisiana brigade","value":"323 casualties among about 550 men, about 59 percent","note":"The episode rounds the brigade’s loss to 61 percent. National Park Service figures list 323 men shot down and every regimental commander hit during about 30 minutes of fighting."},{"label":"Immediate outcome","value":"A stalemate in the eastern Cornfield and East Woods","note":"By about 7 a.m. Ricketts’s division and Lawton’s front-line brigades were badly damaged. Stephen D. Lee’s guns had helped disrupt the Federal advance, and neither side had secured a decisive advantage in this sector."},{"label":"Senior leader casualties in scope","value":"Col. Marcellus Douglass killed; Brig. Gen. George L. Hartsuff wounded","note":"Douglass was killed while commanding Lawton’s brigade south of the Cornfield. Hartsuff’s wound delayed his brigade until Col. Richard Coulter assumed command."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“The Bloodiest Day: The Battle of Antietam” by Ronald H. Bailey","note":"The volume belongs to the illustrated Time-Life Civil War series and was recommended as an accessible introduction, especially for younger or newer readers."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Ewell, Poffenberger, Mumma, Hartsuff, Hays, Douglass, Dunker Church, J. E. B. Stuart, A. W. Garber, and John Carroll Delaney","note":"The automated transcript produces phonetic forms including “Yule,” “Poffenberg,” “Mama,” “Hartseff,” “Hayes,” “Douglas,” “Dunkirk Church,” “Jeb Stewart,” “A. W. Gerber,” and “John Delancey.” This guide uses the standard historical spellings."}],"maps":[{"title":"The northern battlefield at daybreak","caption":"Hooker arranged I Corps with Doubleday on the right, Ricketts on the left, and most of Meade’s division in support; Seymour already occupied part of the East Woods. Across the front, Jackson placed Jones near the West Woods and Lawton south and east of the Cornfield, with Hood in reserve. Confederate batteries held the Dunker Church plateau and Nicodemus Heights. Positions and terrain are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield Cornfield Trail, U.S. Army Center of Military History staff ride guide, Official Records, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep191-daybreak-deployment.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the northern Antietam battlefield at daybreak, showing Hooker’s First Corps north of Miller’s Cornfield, Confederate infantry south of the Cornfield and in the West Woods, and artillery on Nicodemus Heights and by the Dunker Church."},{"title":"Ricketts’s piecemeal attack, about 6–7 a.m.","caption":"Duryea advanced through the Cornfield first and met Douglass’s Georgians at its southern edge. Walker shifted west from the Mumma farm to add converging fire. Hartsuff’s brigade, led forward by Coulter after Hartsuff was wounded, and Christian’s brigade, reorganized under Lyle, arrived separately. Hays’s Louisiana brigade counterattacked into the eastern Cornfield. Movements are generalized and compressed. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield Cornfield Trail, Ricketts’s, Walker’s, Hays’s, Jackson’s, and Lee’s Official Reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep191-ricketts-attack.svg","alt":"Schematic tactical map showing Duryea, Coulter, and Lyle advancing separately through the Cornfield and East Woods while Douglass and Walker fire from the south and east and Hays counterattacks north."},{"title":"Knap’s Pennsylvania battery after the battle","caption":"Alexander Gardner photographed Capt. Joseph M. Knap’s Independent Pennsylvania Battery E approaching the Antietam battlefield after the battle, with a dead horse in the foreground. The photograph appeared on the official episode page and documents the artillery landscape described in the episode; it does not show the opening cannonade in progress.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep191-knaps-battery-antietam.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner stereograph of Knap’s Pennsylvania Independent Battery E with artillery vehicles and soldiers on the Antietam battlefield and a dead horse in the foreground."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:12","audioTime":72,"title":"The night-before position is recapped","detail":"Hooker’s I Corps has crossed the Antietam and Seymour’s brigade holds part of the East Woods after a dusk fight with Hood."},{"time":"2:27","audioTime":147,"title":"Mansfield crosses in rain and darkness","detail":"XII Corps moves across the creek after nightfall and bivouacs roughly a mile behind and left of Hooker."},{"time":"3:01","audioTime":181,"title":"Hood goes into reserve","detail":"Jackson permits Hood’s exhausted brigades to pull back and cook, warning that they must return at the double if called."},{"time":"5:45","audioTime":345,"title":"A Wisconsin soldier waits for dawn","detail":"William F. Goodhue’s recollection describes wet muskets, fog, abandoned breakfasts, and the expectation of immediate battle."},{"time":"7:39","audioTime":459,"title":"Blackford describes civilians under the guns","detail":"William W. Blackford recalls helping women and children cross a plowed field while the opposing batteries temporarily hold their fire."},{"time":"10:38","audioTime":638,"title":"Hooker chooses the Dunker Church plateau","detail":"From the picket line shortly after 5 a.m., Hooker identifies the raised ground and Confederate guns near the church as his objective."},{"time":"12:41","audioTime":761,"title":"The I Corps plan is laid out","detail":"Doubleday will advance along the Hagerstown Turnpike, Ricketts through the Cornfield and East Woods, and Meade in support."},{"time":"13:47","audioTime":827,"title":"I Corps attacks without coordinated support","detail":"Hooker’s roughly 8,600 men open the battle before a working plan has been arranged with Mansfield’s XII Corps."},{"time":"15:00","audioTime":900,"title":"Hooker acts as a corps commander","detail":"The episode argues that Hooker organizes his own divisions first and only then sends Mansfield a general instruction to support him."},{"time":"17:28","audioTime":1048,"title":"Jackson’s strength and line are defined","detail":"About 7,700 Confederates occupy the northern battlefield under Jackson, including Lawton’s and Jones’s divisions and Hood’s reserve."},{"time":"18:14","audioTime":1094,"title":"The Mumma farmhouse burns","detail":"Confederates torch the building to deny it to Federal sharpshooters, creating a column of smoke over the eastern battlefield."},{"time":"18:58","audioTime":1138,"title":"Stephen D. Lee masses guns by the church","detail":"Four Confederate batteries on the plateau east of the Dunker Church command the approaches from the north and east."},{"time":"19:17","audioTime":1157,"title":"Nicodemus Heights threatens Hooker’s flank","detail":"Stuart and Pelham place 14 guns on the commanding ridge, supported by Early’s infantry brigade."},{"time":"20:24","audioTime":1224,"title":"The opening cannonade begins","detail":"Garber later claims the first shot; soon after 5 a.m. artillery on both sides exchanges fire through fog and overcast light."},{"time":"21:44","audioTime":1304,"title":"Seymour renews the East Woods fight","detail":"Pennsylvania Reserves advance to the southern edge of the woods and engage Walker’s brigade on the Mumma farm."},{"time":"23:25","audioTime":1405,"title":"Ricketts and Doubleday form for attack","detail":"Duryea leads Ricketts’s left-wing advance while Gibbon’s brigade spearheads Doubleday’s movement on the right."},{"time":"24:41","audioTime":1481,"title":"Duryea enters the Cornfield","detail":"After Federal guns fire precautionary canister, about 1,100 men move into the head-high corn at roughly 6 a.m."},{"time":"25:15","audioTime":1515,"title":"Douglass’s Georgians rise and fire","detail":"The concealed Confederate line delivers a close volley as Duryea’s brigade emerges from the southern edge of the corn."},{"time":"26:08","audioTime":1568,"title":"Walker adds converging fire","detail":"Trimble’s brigade shifts from the Mumma farm to a rocky ledge and strikes Duryea from the east while Seymour’s remnants fire into Walker’s flank."},{"time":"27:21","audioTime":1641,"title":"Ricketts’s reinforcements break down","detail":"Hartsuff is wounded, Coulter takes over his brigade, and Christian abandons the other supporting brigade."},{"time":"28:03","audioTime":1683,"title":"A fourteen-year-old rallies the colors","detail":"Private John Carroll Delaney helps recover the fallen flags of the 107th Pennsylvania during Duryea’s withdrawal."},{"time":"30:17","audioTime":1817,"title":"Hays’s Louisianans counterattack","detail":"The Louisiana brigade charges across the pasture and collides with Coulter’s Federal brigade near the Cornfield and East Woods."},{"time":"32:15","audioTime":1935,"title":"The 12th Massachusetts is devastated","detail":"The episode counts 224 casualties and a 67 percent loss rate after the regiment’s fight against Hays and Douglass."},{"time":"32:29","audioTime":1949,"title":"Thompson pushes a battery into the corn","detail":"Federal artillery occupies a small rise inside the Cornfield and contributes to the converging fire that stops the Confederate counterattack."},{"time":"33:11","audioTime":1991,"title":"Douglass is killed","detail":"The Confederate colonel falls while every regimental commander in Hays’s brigade is also down."},{"time":"33:40","audioTime":2020,"title":"Lyle’s brigade finally enters","detail":"At about 7 a.m., Peter Lyle leads Christian’s former brigade through the East Woods and relieves Coulter’s shattered formations."},{"time":"34:34","audioTime":2074,"title":"The loss rates are compared","detail":"The episode contrasts the 12th Massachusetts at 67 percent with the Louisiana brigade at roughly 61 percent."},{"time":"34:50","audioTime":2090,"title":"The eastern attack ends in stalemate","detail":"Ricketts’s separate brigade attacks fail to break Lawton’s position, and Confederate artillery has prevented Federal guns from dominating the field."},{"time":"35:53","audioTime":2153,"title":"The book recommendation closes the episode","detail":"The hosts recommend Ronald H. Bailey’s illustrated Time-Life volume “The Bloodiest Day: The Battle of Antietam.”"}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Directed Hooker’s I Corps to attack the Confederate left and sent Mansfield’s XII Corps to support it"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker","command":"I Corps and northern wing","subordinates":"Selected the Dunker Church plateau as the objective and ordered his three divisions forward at daybreak"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Abner Doubleday","command":"1st Division, I Corps","subordinates":"Advanced on the corps right along the Hagerstown Turnpike; John Gibbon’s brigade led the movement discussed next episode"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. James B. Ricketts","command":"2nd Division, I Corps","subordinates":"Sent Duryea, Hartsuff, and Christian forward through the Cornfield and East Woods, but the brigades entered separately"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Abram Duryea","command":"1st Brigade, Ricketts’s division","subordinates":"Led the first major Federal infantry advance through the Cornfield and withdrew after severe losses and low ammunition"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George L. Hartsuff / Col. Richard Coulter","command":"3rd Brigade, Ricketts’s division","subordinates":"Coulter assumed command after a shell fragment wounded Hartsuff and brought the delayed brigade into the fight"},{"commander":"Col. William A. Christian / Col. Peter Lyle","command":"2nd Brigade, Ricketts’s division","subordinates":"Lyle took command after Christian fled and finally advanced through the East Woods at about 7 a.m."},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George G. Meade","command":"3rd Division, I Corps, Pennsylvania Reserves","subordinates":"Supported the center while Truman Seymour’s brigade renewed the fight from its overnight position in the East Woods"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield","command":"XII Corps","subordinates":"Moved forward to support Hooker, but received little advance coordination and did not enter the episode’s main action"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#963b31","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Held the army’s left at Sharpsburg under Jackson and accepted battle against the larger Army of the Potomac"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Confederate left wing","subordinates":"Controlled the northern defense, including Ewell’s and Jackson’s divisions, Hood’s reserve, and Stuart on the flank"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Alexander R. Lawton","command":"Ewell’s division","subordinates":"Deployed the brigades of Lawton, Trimble, Hays, and Early across the eastern front and in reserve"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. John R. Jones","command":"Jackson’s division","subordinates":"Held the West Woods sector north of the Dunker Church at the beginning of the battle"},{"commander":"Col. Marcellus Douglass","command":"Lawton’s brigade","subordinates":"Commanded the Georgians south of the Cornfield and was killed during the fight against Ricketts’s division"},{"commander":"Col. James A. Walker","command":"Trimble’s brigade","subordinates":"Defended the Mumma farm line and shifted part of the brigade west to fire into Duryea’s flank"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Harry T. Hays","command":"Louisiana brigade","subordinates":"Counterattacked toward the eastern Cornfield and suffered the loss of every regimental commander"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. John Bell Hood","command":"Hood’s division","subordinates":"Waited in the West Woods reserve after his exhausted brigades withdrew from the night picket line"},{"commander":"Col. Stephen D. Lee","command":"Artillery battalion","subordinates":"Massed four batteries on the plateau east of the Dunker Church and fired into the Federal approach"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. J. E. B. Stuart / Capt. John Pelham","command":"Cavalry and horse artillery on Nicodemus Heights","subordinates":"Placed flank fire on Hooker’s advance; Jubal Early’s infantry brigade protected the commanding ridge"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Joseph Hooker","role":"I Corps commander and de facto northern wing commander who chose the Dunker Church plateau as his objective","side":"United States","source":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide-stop-1.htm"},{"name":"Joseph K. F. Mansfield","role":"New XII Corps commander ordered to support Hooker without detailed coordination before the attack","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/the-east-woods.htm"},{"name":"James B. Ricketts","role":"I Corps division commander whose three brigades attacked through the eastern Cornfield and East Woods","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=49"},{"name":"Abram Duryea","role":"Led Ricketts’s first brigade through the Cornfield at about 6 a.m.","side":"United States","source":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide-stop-1.htm"},{"name":"George L. Hartsuff","role":"Brigade commander wounded by a shell fragment before his troops could closely support Duryea","side":"United States","source":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/6generals.htm"},{"name":"Richard Coulter","role":"Assumed Hartsuff’s brigade and led it into the Cornfield–East Woods fight","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=49"},{"name":"Peter Lyle","role":"Took command of Christian’s brigade and brought it into action through the East Woods at about 7 a.m.","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=49"},{"name":"John Carroll Delaney","role":"Fourteen-year-old private in the 107th Pennsylvania who helped recover the regiment’s colors during Duryea’s withdrawal","side":"United States"},{"name":"Alexander R. Lawton","role":"Led Ewell’s division on Jackson’s eastern front south of the Cornfield","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=41"},{"name":"Marcellus Douglass","role":"Colonel commanding Lawton’s brigade; directed the first volleys against Duryea and was killed in the fighting","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide-stop-2.htm"},{"name":"James A. Walker","role":"Colonel commanding Trimble’s brigade on the Mumma farm and eastern side of the Confederate line","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=179"},{"name":"Harry T. Hays","role":"Led the Louisiana brigade’s counterattack against Coulter near the Cornfield and East Woods","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=180"},{"name":"Stephen D. Lee","role":"Artillery battalion commander whose four batteries occupied the Dunker Church plateau","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide-stop-1.htm"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Cavalry commander responsible for the Confederate left flank and its artillery on Nicodemus Heights","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=41"},{"name":"John Pelham","role":"Horse artillery commander who placed guns on Nicodemus Heights","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=41"},{"name":"William W. Blackford","role":"Stuart staff officer whose recollection describes escorting civilians to safety during the opening cannonade","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Col. Marcellus Douglass","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander of Lawton’s brigade, Ewell’s division","outcome":"Killed while directing his Georgians in the fighting south of the Cornfield; the National Park Service account says rifle fire wounded him before an artillery shell killed him."},{"name":"Brig. Gen. George L. Hartsuff","side":"United States","role":"Commander of 3rd Brigade, Ricketts’s division, I Corps","outcome":"Wounded by a shell fragment before his brigade entered the main fight; Col. Richard Coulter assumed command and led it forward."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep191-daybreak-deployment.svg","alt":"Schematic daybreak deployment map of the northern Antietam battlefield.","caption":"Hooker’s I Corps faced Jackson’s line across Miller’s Cornfield, with Confederate artillery commanding both the center and the Federal right flank.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Official Records, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript."},{"image":"/images/ep191-ricketts-attack.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Ricketts’s piecemeal advance and the Confederate response.","caption":"Duryea, Coulter, and Lyle reached the fight separately, allowing Douglass, Walker, Hays, and the Confederate batteries to meet each threat in sequence.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, Official Reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript."},{"image":"/images/ep191-knaps-battery-antietam.jpg","alt":"Knap’s Pennsylvania Independent Battery E on the Antietam battlefield after the battle.","caption":"Alexander Gardner’s September 1862 stereograph of Knap’s Pennsylvania battery, the historical image featured on the official episode page.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 191 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/04/16/civil-war-podcast-episode-191/"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Cornfield Trail introduction and map","url":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Cornfield action timeline","url":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide-stop-1.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Confederate battle line and Douglass","url":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide-stop-2.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Confederate counterattacks and Hays’s losses","url":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide-stop-3.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: East Woods","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/the-east-woods.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: 12th Massachusetts at the Cornfield","url":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/cornfield-trail-guide-stop-6.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: casualties by battlefield sector","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/casualties.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: generals killed and wounded","url":"https://home.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/6generals.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: Antietam staff ride guide","url":"https://history.army.mil/Portals/143/Images/Publications/Staff%20Rides/PNG/cmhPub_35-3-1.pdf"},{"label":"Official Records, Series I, Volume XIX, Part I","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/027"},{"label":"Primary source: James B. Ricketts’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=49"},{"label":"Primary source: James A. Walker’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=179"},{"label":"Primary source: Harry T. Hays’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=180"},{"label":"Primary source: Thomas J. Jackson’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=41"},{"label":"Primary source: Robert E. Lee’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=15"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Atlas of the battlefield of Antietam","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/map05000006/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Knap’s Pennsylvania Independent Battery E","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/stereo.1s02770/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Knap’s Pennsylvania battery after the battle",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep191-knaps-battery-antietam.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Knap’s Pennsylvania battery after the battle at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep191-knaps-battery-antietam.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner stereograph of Knap’s Pennsylvania Independent Battery E with artillery vehicles and soldiers on the Antietam battlefield and a dead horse in the foreground.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Knap’s Pennsylvania battery after the battle"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Alexander Gardner photographed Capt. Joseph M. Knap’s Independent Pennsylvania Battery E approaching the Antietam battlefield after the battle, with a dead horse in the foreground. The photograph appeared on the official episode page and documents the artillery landscape described in the episode; it does not show the opening cannonade in progress."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep191-knaps-battery-antietam.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Directed Hooker’s I Corps to attack the Confederate left and sent Mansfield’s XII Corps to support it"}]]}],["$","div","I Corps and northern wing-Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"I Corps and northern wing"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Selected the Dunker Church plateau as the objective and ordered his three divisions forward at daybreak"}]]}],["$","div","1st Division, I Corps-Brig. Gen. Abner Doubleday",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"1st Division, I Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Abner Doubleday"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Advanced on the corps right along the Hagerstown Turnpike; John Gibbon’s brigade led the movement discussed next episode"}]]}],["$","div","2nd Division, I Corps-Brig. Gen. James B. Ricketts",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"2nd Division, I Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. James B. Ricketts"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Sent Duryea, Hartsuff, and Christian forward through the Cornfield and East Woods, but the brigades entered separately"}]]}],["$","div","1st Brigade, Ricketts’s division-Brig. Gen. Abram Duryea",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"1st Brigade, Ricketts’s division"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Abram Duryea"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Led the first major Federal infantry advance through the Cornfield and withdrew after severe losses and low ammunition"}]]}],["$","div","3rd Brigade, Ricketts’s division-Brig. Gen. George L. Hartsuff / Col. Richard Coulter",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"3rd Brigade, Ricketts’s division"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. George L. Hartsuff / Col. Richard Coulter"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Coulter assumed command after a shell fragment wounded Hartsuff and brought the delayed brigade into the fight"}]]}],["$","div","2nd Brigade, Ricketts’s division-Col. William A. Christian / Col. Peter Lyle",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"2nd Brigade, Ricketts’s division"}],"$L20","$L21"]}],"$L22","$L23"]]}],"$L24"]}],"$L25"]]]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"losses","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Important leaders killed or wounded"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"loss-list","children":[["$","article","Col. Marcellus Douglass",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side confederate","children":"Confederacy"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Col. Marcellus Douglass"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander of Lawton’s brigade, Ewell’s division","."]}]," ","Killed while directing his Georgians in the fighting south of the Cornfield; the National Park Service account says rifle fire wounded him before an artillery shell killed him."]}]]}],["$","article","Brig. Gen. George L. Hartsuff",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. George L. Hartsuff"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander of 3rd Brigade, Ricketts’s division, I Corps","."]}]," ","Wounded by a shell fragment before his brigade entered the main fight; Col. Richard Coulter assumed command and led it forward."]}]]}]]}]]}]
16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L26","/images/ep191-knaps-battery-antietam.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep191-knaps-battery-antietam.jpg","alt":"Knap’s Pennsylvania Independent Battery E on the Antietam battlefield after the battle.","caption":"Alexander Gardner’s September 1862 stereograph of Knap’s Pennsylvania battery, the historical image featured on the official episode page.","name":"Knap’s Pennsylvania Independent Battery E on the Antietam battlefield after the battle.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."}]]}]]}]
